A||||17||Formula88||Pics of 245/50-15s?||||5||
Z||000000||Formula88||07-20-2001||12:33 AM||||Has anyone put 245/50-15's on the back of their Fiero, preferable on the stock cross lace wheels?  I'd like to see a pic to see how they look in the wheel well.  I'm going to be getting some new tires soon, and I'm torn between Bridgestone Potenza RE950's in the stock sizes or Dunlop SP8000's in 225/50-15 front and 245/50-15 rear.  According to TireRack.com, they'll fit the stock rims fine.  I just want to see how they'll look.   <P>Any help is appreciated.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||07-20-2001||05:44 AM||||I think 245s would be pushing it.  I have 225/50R15s on all 4 corners of 4Play and it looks close.  I'll try to take some pics of that when I get home from work this afternoon.  4Play's wearing stock GT/lace rims, too.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||07-20-2001||09:17 AM||||Thanks.  I'd like to see that.  My reasoning behind the 245s on the back is that since mine is an 88, the front and rear wheels are different widths.  I don't want to run the same size tire all the way around on different width wheels.  That would make the front and rear tires perform differently.  <P>But let me see how the 225s look.  If they're right on the edge, then the 245s would likely stick out too far.  I don't want to get the Ricer steam roller wheel look!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000003||AusFiero||07-20-2001||11:59 AM||||245s would be dangerous. 225s and 215s is the combo your after I think.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||07-20-2001||12:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AusFiero:<BR><B>245s would be dangerous. 225s and 215s is the combo your after I think.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How would 245s be dangerous?  According to Tire Racks spec sheet, the Measured Rim Width for a 245/50-15 is a 7" rim.  That's what I have (rear).  The acceptable range is 6.5"-9.5".  The Measured rim with on the 225/50-15s is a 6" rim, so that should be ok on the front.  The acceptable range for the 225s is 6"-8".<P>Now, it may not look right, that's why I want the pics, but how would it be dangerous?  I really do appreciate your input.  If I'm missing something here, please let me know.<P>215/50s aren't available and a 225/50 on the back is much shorter thatn the 215/60s from the factory.  I'm trying to keep this as close to stock tire height as I can.  Ideally, a 235/55-15 on the back would be perfect, but no one makes one.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000005||LT1 Chop Top||07-20-2001||12:31 PM||||I have 255-40-17 (17x9) rear and 225-45-16 (16x7.5) front on my 88 GT.  No rubbing at all, and SUPER handling!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||07-20-2001||01:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LT1 Chop Top:<BR><B>I have 255-40-17 (17x9) rear and 225-45-16 (16x7.5) front on my 88 GT.  No rubbing at all, and SUPER handling!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And they fit inside the wheelwells?  No sticking out?  My big concern is how they'll look on factory 15" rims.  The offset can change with tht aftermarket rims.  I'm looking to find out if the 245s on 15" stock rims will stick out of the fender.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||prtzlboy||07-20-2001||07:05 PM||||I'm running BFG 235/60s on my 14" tech rims in the rear and they work fine and look sweet.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tigger||07-20-2001||07:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>How would 245s be dangerous?  According to Tire Racks spec sheet, the Measured Rim Width for a 245/50-15 is a 7" rim.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>245's will fit a 7" rim, but the sidewalls will "pooch" out from the lip of the rim.  Enough maybe to rub against the strut in a static condition and/or with dynamic deflections of the sidewall under cornering  causing it to rub on the strut, brake line, or something else.  I don't think the stock wheels have enough offset.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||07-20-2001||07:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tigger:<BR><B> 245's will fit a 7" rim, but the sidewalls will "pooch" out from the lip of the rim.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Again, this doesn't make sense, since a 7" rim is the standard rim width listed by Tire Rack for that size tire.  According to their website:<P><I><FONT COLOR="blue">"The measuring rim width is the industry standardized rim width upon which the tire must be mounted in order to confirm it meets its dimensional targets. "</I></FONT><P>Everyone here is talking like it's way too big for the rim, but Tire Rack is saying that's the width it was designed for.  Is Tire Rack wrong?||||reg||4
Z||000010||AusFiero||07-20-2001||08:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> How would 245s be dangerous?  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Obviously we have different government road safety standards in Australia. 235 is the maximum allowed in this country on a 7" rim. If you have to much side wall bulging out you can roll the tyre off the rim cornering at high speed. <P>Have seen it happen too. Not a pretty site as the car usually ends up upside down.<P>Unless you have 245s specifically designed for the rim size, then go for it. I know ours aren't. <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||07-21-2001||01:01 AM||||I'm not saying it's the best idea, or trying to be stubborn.  But people are saying it's a bad idea, when the alleged "experts" are saying otherwise.  But it's becoming a moot point anyway, because I'm getting the Potenza RE950's tomorrow and they only come in the stock sizes.  I'll have to wait until I can get new rims to get oversize tires.  Then I'm going with Bridgestone Potenza RE730's or some Potenza S-03 Pole Position's, depending on how much moola I can part with.  I still haven't found a rim I like, in the size I want, that's not over $500 each.||||reg||6
Z||000012||Formula88||07-21-2001||01:34 PM||||Well, I just got 2 of the Bridgestone Potenza RE950's for the front of my beast, in stock sizes.  So far, they seem pretty awesome, but since I still have crap on the back until next month, I can't really tell.  They sure do look good, though!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000013||California Kid||07-21-2001||02:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>I just want to see how they'll look.   <P>Any help is appreciated.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The picture below might help give you an how well the 245X50X15 is going to pack on your 7 in rim.  The rims I'm using are 7.5 X16 with 245X50X16 T/A VR Radials. <P>Just my opinion but I think you'd really be forcing the sidewalls in took far with the 7 inch rims. I consider mine to be acceptable for road course racing, but if the walls were pulled in more???<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Tires.jpg"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Dragon||07-23-2001||10:21 PM||||I've been running 225 x 50 on front and 245 x 50 on the rear for years and like the way it handles and looks.  I don't know if the different sizes front to back help much on the steering.  But it looks meaner.<P>I did put up a page with closer shots of the present tires and rims on the Emerald Dragon.<BR> <A HREF="http://SpaceCoastFieros.com/dragonwheels/" TARGET=_blank>http://SpaceCoastFieros.com/dragonwheels/</A> <P>I doubt I will go back to smaller rubber again.<P>------------------<BR>Dragon<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://spacecoastfieros.com/dragon.jpg">  <P>   Space Coast Fieros||||reg||10
Z||000015||Philphine||07-23-2001||11:22 PM||||cal. kid, dose what you have fit without going to coilovers? i have 16x7.5" rims and 255/50-16 on the rear of my '87 and need to switch to coilovers. i have a similar set of wheels for an '86 and know where i can get a set of 245/50-16's. i would like to avoid needing coilovers for it too. i thought i might have to try for 245/45-16's but they are harder to find deals on.<P>same question to dragon. just read your post and looked at your site. how wide are your wheels and did you switch to coilovers?<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>SERENITY NOW!!!!<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 07-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||California Kid||07-23-2001||11:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B>cal. kid, dose what you have fit without going to coilovers? <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Using stock springs (no rubbing), just slightly shortened to lower 1.5 inch. No problem with clearance, wheels have a 5 3/8 inch offset.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||California Kid||07-24-2001||12:05 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B> [QUOTE]Originally posted by Philphine:<BR>[b]cal. kid, dose what you have fit without going to coilovers? <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Using stock springs (no rubbing), just slightly shortened to lower 1.5 inch. No problem with clearance, wheels have a 5 3/8 inch offset.<P>[/B][/QUOTE]<P>Scratch that comment about me using stock springs, they are Koni modified (shortened). Sorry about that, it's late and I'm going to bed.<BR>||||reg||1
